B6 — Sales Performance Diagnosis
1️⃣ Problem Clarification

• Leadership asked to build an ML model to identify underperforming teams.
• The real issue is defining underperformance relative to structural context.
• This is a diagnostic problem, not a leaderboard exercise.
• Decision-makers: Sales leadership, Regional directors, RevOps.
• Decisions informed: intervention, resource allocation, quota adjustments.
Plain-English Problem Statement

• Estimate expected sales performance using structural factors, then identify regions or teams that significantly over- or under-perform relative to that expectation, and explain why.
2️⃣ Problem Type Classification

• Primary: Regression modeling + residual analysis.
• Supporting: Anomaly detection and clustering for peer grouping.
• Not appropriate: Raw revenue ranking or black-box classification.
• Focus: Fair, context-adjusted performance evaluation.
3️⃣ Data Science Scope

• Revenue, quota attainment, pipeline size, win rate, deal size.
• Marketing spend, headcount, rep tenure, product mix.
• Structural controls: market size, economic conditions, seasonality.
• Granularity: Region-level, team-level, time-series.
• Risks: Simpson’s paradox, leakage, survivorship bias, quota distortion.
4️⃣ Modeling Approach

Phase 0 — EDA First

• Inspect distributions, trends, and structural differences before modeling.
Phase 1 — Expected Performance Model

• Use linear regression baseline to estimate expected revenue.
• Include structural predictors (pipeline, market size, headcount, etc.).
Phase 2 — Residual Analysis

• Residual = Actual − Expected.
• Negative residuals indicate underperformance relative to context.
• Interpret results with R² and diagnostic checks.
Phase 3 — Anomaly Detection / Peer Groups

• Use clustering to group structurally similar regions.
• Evaluate performance within peer groups for fairness.
Explicit Non-Goals

• No opaque black-box models.
• No labeling teams without structural adjustment.
• No single-metric simplification.
5️⃣ Concrete Evaluation Plan

• 1. Define structural control variables.
• 2. Build regression baseline model.
• 3. Validate model diagnostics and residual behavior.
• 4. Identify statistically meaningful deviations.
• 5. Cluster peer regions and re-evaluate within groups.
• 6. Conduct sensitivity checks.
• 7. Deliver explanation-first report with drivers of underperformance.
6️⃣ Use of LLMs

• Used to enumerate structural confounders and alternative framings.
• Rejected default suggestion of classification-based labeling.
• Maintained focus on fairness-adjusted residual diagnostics.
7️⃣ Unknown Methods Discovery

• Peer-based performance modeling through clustering.
• Corrected assumption: Low revenue does not equal underperformance.
• Underperformance must be measured relative to structural expectation.
